00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  if you plucked people from all across  the country and said we have a farm the  image that they have of who's that  farmer  would not be kristen leach  [Music]  my name is kristen leach and i'm a  farmer in winters california  and i grow predominantly korean and east  asian herbs and vegetables  i was born in tegu korea  i was adopted as an infant i grew up on  long island in new york  the regional cuisine of long island is  pizza and bagels so that's what i was  just sort of raised on  i just had never really explored korean  heritage but i did get curious about the  food  oh wait this is a really good  little one i worked on organic farms  when i was a teenager  and so i just thought like oh i know how  to grow plants maybe i should grow some  of these plants from korea just to  better understand this place where i'm  from  i  went back to korea for the first time  since i left  i spent just like three weeks traveling  around and was learning from all of  these farmers and visiting different  farms  it felt like a pretty profound way to  actually engage with identity  because it answered for me a sense of  what place i could find within like a  bigger story of korean history  [Music]  i practice a style of farming that's  referred to as natural farming in korea  and japan  everything on the farm has grown  organically  and essentially it's the way just like  peasant subsistence farmers farm  some of the heritage crops that we focus  on here are the korean sesame leaf  the flavor is kind of licoricey  and minty  chame korean melon  is like somewhere between like a  cucumber and melon in flavor it's really  really crunchy  chili peppers a really beloved staple  and japanese eggplant just a small  baseball sized eggplant it's super dense  and really creamy  [Music]  three times a week i'll usually head to  the bay to make a delivery  namugaji is a restaurant in the mission  district in san francisco  all of the produce goes directly to  their restaurant  and their chefs and cooks figure out how  to kind of design a menu around it  my name is dennis lee i'm the chef and  owner of non mugaji in san francisco  [Music]  right now we're doing a really nice  simple grilled eggplant dish with a  really heavy dusting of minced perilla  we're doing a nice melon salad right now  with watermelon and korean melon we have  a chili oil that we serve with our ramen  that has korean chilies from the farm  it's a restaurant that's operated by  korean americans so you see that in what  we do  the way that our relationship works with  kristin is totally unique because it's  tied to who she is  to be able to support an asian woman  and co-create a platform to express  ourselves and share a story with people  that's pretty special  the food that dennis is making allows  itself to just be really original and it  doesn't feel like it's trying to just  reproduce  a mold of what is korean or what is  asian american  i'm learning about like my lineage i'm  trying to figure out a sense of  belonging  the experience of adoption is so  complicated i feel really grateful to  have farming be the way that i interact  with my culture  [Music]

